Sections "COLLECTION" contain missmatched items: {'COLLECTION_SUMMARY': ["Individuals living with severe obesity were recruited and signed an informed consent to participate in the study. Patients with alcohol abuse, viral and autoimmune hepatitis, immunosuppressive or steroid use were excluded. Liver core, peripheral blood, and omental white adipose tissue (WAT) biopsies were obtained at time of bariatric surgery per standard clinical protocols, and immediately flash-frozen and stored in liquid N2 until metabolimics analysis. Liver core biopsies were sectioned and stained using hematoxylin and eosin and Masson trichrome stains, and subsequently scored using the pediatric MASH-CRN scoring system by a single, clinical liver pathologist (Dr. Sara Szabo) experienced in application of this scoring system. The MASLD activity score (MAS) is a sum of scores for steatosis, lobular inflammation and hepatocyte ballooning. Fibrosis was separately staged. Individuals with MASLD (without the presence of type 2 diabetes) were segregated into those with MASL (n=6) and those with MASH (n=21), based on the well-established clinical parameters of hepatocellular disease. In addition, participant demographics and clinical characteristics including levels of serum triglyceride, aminotransferase (ALT, AST), and HbA1C were obtained. Participant recruitment and all study protocols were approved by the institutional review board at Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center (IRB 2016-1622, 2019-1331).", "Individuals living with severe obesity were recruited and signed an informed consent to participate in the study. Patients with alcohol abuse, viral and autoimmune hepatitis, immunosuppressive or steroid use were excluded. Liver core, peripheral blood, and omental white adipose tissue (WAT) biopsies were obtained at time of bariatric surgery per standard clinical protocols, and immediately flash-frozen and stored in liquid N2 until metabolimics analysis. Liver core biopsies were sectioned and stained using hematoxylin and eosin and Masson trichrome stains, and subsequently scored using the pediatric MASH-CRN scoring system by a single, clinical liver pathologist (Dr. Sara Szabo) experienced in application of this scoring system. The MASLD activity score (MAS) is a sum of scores for steatosis, lobular inflammation and hepatocyte ballooning. Fibrosis was separately staged. Individuals with MASLD (without the presence of type 2 diabetes) were segregated into those with MASL (n=6) and those with MASH (n=21), based on the well-established clinical parameters of hepatocellular disease. In addition, participant demographics and clinical characteristics including levels of serum triglyceride, aminotransferase (ALT, AST), and HbA1C were obtained. Participant recruitment and all study protocols were approved by the institutional review board at Cincinnati Children''s Hospital Medical Center (IRB 2016-1622, 2019-1331)."]}
